JSX allows us to write HTML-like code inside JavaScript.

Without JSX :-
const element = React.createElement(
  "h1",
  null,
  "Hello Arun"
);

With JSX :-
const element = <h1>Hello Arun</h1>;

Simple React Component :-
function App() {
  return <h1>Hello Arun</h1>;
}
export default App;

Here :- <h1>Hello Arun</h1> is JSX.



[JSX Syntax]
------------
JSX looks similar to HTML, but there are some differences.

Basic Syntax :-
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello Arun</h1>
      <p>Welcome to React</p>
    </div>
  );
}

Output :-
Hello Arun
Welcome to React

Important : JSX should normally have one parent element.

✅ Correct:
return (
  <div>
    <h1>Hello</h1>
    <p>Welcome</p>
  </div>
);

❌ Incorrect:
return (
  <h1>Hello</h1>
  <p>Welcome</p>
);

Because there are two separate elements → <h1> & <p>
React expects a single parent. We can solve this using a Fragment.



[JSX Attributes]
----------------
JSX uses attributes similar to HTML.

function App() {
  return (
    <img
      src="https://example.com/image.jpg"
      alt="Example"
    />
  );
}

But some HTML attributes have different names in JSX.
HTML → <div class="container">
JSX → <div className="container">
Because class is a JavaScript keyword, React uses → className

Another common example :-
HTML → <label for="name">
JSX → <label htmlFor="name">

Common JSX attributes :-
className
htmlFor
onClick
onChange
tabIndex



[JSX Expressions]
-----------------
We can write JavaScript expressions inside JSX using → { }

Example1 ➔ Variables :-
function App() {
  const name = "Arun";
  return <h1>Hello {name}</h1>;
}
export default App;

Output :-
Hello Arun

Here the {name} is a JSX expression.

Example2 ➔ Variables :-
function App() {
  const age = 25;
  return <p>My age is {age}</p>;
}
export default App;

Output :-
My age is 25

Example3 ➔ Variables :-
function App() {
  const name = "Arun";
  const age = 25;
  const salary = 50000;
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Name: {name}</h1>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
      <p>Salary: {salary}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;

Output :-
Name: Arun
Age: 25
Salary: 50000

Example4 ➔ Calculations :-
function App() {
  const a = 10;
  const b = 20;
  return <h1>Total: {a + b}</h1>;
}
export default App;

Output :-
Total: 30

Example5 ➔ Function calls :-
function App() {
  function getName() {
    return "Arun";
  }
  return <h1>Hello {getName()}</h1>;
}
export default App;

Output :-
Hello Arun

Example6 ➔ Ternary Operator :-
function App() {
  const age = 25;
  return (
    <h1>
      {age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor"}
    </h1>
  );
}
export default App;

Output :-
Adult

Example7 ➔ Conditional Rendering using && :-
function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome</h1>
      {isLoggedIn && <p>You are logged in</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;

Output :-
Welcome
You are logged in

Example8 ➔ Conditional Rendering using && :-
function App() {
  const isLoggedIn = false;
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome</h1>
      {isLoggedIn && <p>You are logged in</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;

Output :-
Welcome

So will not render the <p>.

In JSX Expressions can be assign the Variables, Calculations, Function call, Ternary Operator, Array methods.
But remember the Statements cannot directly be used inside JSX {}.

For example, this is not valid :-
{
  if (age >= 18) {
    return "Adult";
  }
}

Instead, use a ternary :-
{age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor"}



[JSX Fragments]
---------------
Sometimes we need to return multiple JSX Elements.
Instead of adding an unnecessary <div>, we can use a Fragment.

Without Fragment :-
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>Welcome</p>
    </div>
  );
}

This creates an extra div in the DOM.
<div>...</div> → creates a <div> element in the DOM.

With Fragment :-
function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>Welcome</p>
    </>
  );
}

<>...</> → does not create an actual HTML element in the DOM.
This is called a Fragment.

We can also write :-
import { Fragment } from "react";
function App() {
  return (
    <Fragment>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>Welcome</p>
    </Fragment>
  );
}

Both are basically used for the same purpose.

Most of the time you'll see :-
<>
  <h1>Hello</h1>
  <p>Welcome</p>
</>

We can use both <div>...</div> or <>...</> (Fragment) to wrap multiple JSX elements.
So <div> </div> creates an actual DOM element, while <> </> does not add an extra element to the DOM.



[JSX Elements]
--------------
JSX element can represent either an HTML element or a React components.
JSX element can represent either an HTML element (<h1>, <div>, <p>, <span>, <button>, <input>, <ul>, <li>) or a React components (<User />).
Multiple JSX elements → wrap them with <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>.

Example1 ➔ only one JSX element being returned, without using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
function App() {
  return <h1>Hello Arun</h1>;
}
export default App;

Example2 ➔ only one JSX element being returned, without using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
function App() {
  return (
    <h1>Hello Arun</h1>
  );
}
export default App;

Example3 ➔ only one JSX element being returned, with using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello Arun</h1>
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;

Example4 ➔ Multiple JSX elements (<h1> and <h2>) are returned, with using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>Hello Arun</h1>
      <h2>Hello Theja</h2>
      <p>Welcome</p>
      <button>Click Me</button>
    </>
  );
}
export default App;

Example5 ➔ So <User /> is React component, only one JSX element without using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
import User from "./Details";
function App() {
  return <User />;
}
export default App;

Example6 ➔ So <User /> is React component, only one JSX element without using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
import User from "./Details";
function App() {
  return (
    <User />
  );
}
export default App;

Example7 ➔ So <User /> is React component, only one JSX element with using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
import User from "./Details";
function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <User />
    </>
  );
}
export default App;

Example8 ➔ So <User /> is React component calling two times, Multiple JSX elements with using a <div>...</div> or Fragment <>...</>
import User from "./Details";
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <User />
      <User />
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;
